commentBody:
@Lewis

I guess because I’ve read so many comments lately suggesting that the world as we know it is ending because crosswords have been besmirched by the use of computers. 

Maybe people are confusing the use of computers as an aid to constructors with asking AI to construct a puzzle.

@SP

Dude.

You make good puzzles.

@SP

Never highlight the rebuses, I say.  

Old school!

@SP

I thought your puzzle was very enjoyable.  It was fun figuring out the trick and the revealer was perfect.  The clue for 28 A was a favorite.  Looking over my recent NYT Thursday solve times, this took longer than most, but not as long as the toughest ones.  

I don't really see any reason why a puzzle like this wouldn't be published by the NYT.  It would certainly be welcomed by me.

@SP
Thank you.  

Actually, it isn't a medal. It's a piece of paper, suitable for framing, but heartfelt, all the same.

@sotto voce 

Today I learned that MacArthur Park has a disco cover!

I only knew about the original by Richard Harris (who also played Dumbledore from the first two Harry Potter movies, fun fact!) and the instrumental cover by Maynard Ferguson. I always thought the song was unique in its style, so it makes sense that there are so many covers of it!

@A

I agree! No references earlier than 1980! Old pop culture is stupid! And tough vocabulary? Letter bank challenges? What is this, a puzzle?!

Boooo!

@Jonathan and @A

No such rule.
Because all the famous oldsters live on in cable TV and streaming services where all the young whipper-snappers can watch and learn.

@A
I'm a millennial, and I know SAL MINEO. I didn't know ELAINE STRITCH, but I could guess her with the crosses.

Also, as has been explained *repeatedly* in the column and the comments, the theme of the puzzle is a letter bank, which is similar to an anagram. The Spelling Bee is a letter bank game. The letter bank also appears frequently in cryptic crosswords.

@Jonathan
Yes. The rule is, they will not know anyone who is dead unless the dead person is younger than the post-boomer being asked. That's because the world was created the day after they were born. Everything before then is a collective hallucination that we have invented to annoy them.
Next question.

@Jennifer
I never know the more recent (later than Snow White and Cinderalla)  "comic book/Disney film" characters, but I'm too lazy to look them up.  Usually they gradually appear....

Is JAH supposed to be Yahweh (shortened and spelt differently)? Or what?

@Mean Old Lady
"Supposed to be"?
Seems like kind of a TUDE there.

@M Aquaman

I wasn’t being snarky. I do think it’s useful to read the column before commenting if you don’t get the theme and just earnestly pointing that out. I’ll be honest, I don’t always read every detail of the column either—but I would if I were stumped. Honestly sorry if you felt that way.

Oh and I do keep saying word bank instead of letter bank, that’s my bad.

@lucky13
And in a similar vein, look up the video of the "Nine to Five" song--by Dolly Parton, so it's all over the internet right now. Ditto the op-ed piece on how Jane Fonda (then pretty toxic as "Hanoi Jane") recruited Dolly for the movie and then Dolly wrote the song. 
  Can you tell that I've done a bit of Dolly- bingeing in the last few days? How had I never heard "Coat of Many Colors"? Yes, pure sentimental sweetness but brought tears anyway.

@MB

Yes.  The biggest difference is that TCM shows its movies from Titles through Credits without interruption. 

In addition, the hosts and guests offer informed anecdotes about the films, directors, and casts.

@Hitch

yeah, I noticed the higher level of US-centricity as I worked through the puzzle. There's always going to be some of that, but more than usual today.
I'm sympathetic towards it as I had difficulty working Canadian and British puzzles when I lived in those places.

@Grant

Some would call "Exodus" less an historical novel and more a whitewashing.

I love historical novels.  But they are not history.

@Anker Lerret
Just the circled letters. Types of pyramids. GREAT in Egypt.  FOOD as in how much of each type of food one should eat. Used to be fats at top, least; fruits and vegetables at bottom, most. Or something along those lines.
HUMAN is something cheerleaders might make. Four on bottom, then 3, then 2, then one on top. Sizes can vary

@Mike
TSK....but then it wouldn't be as confusing to the solvers!  Where's the fun in that?
They don't want to look elsewhere for their fun--such as pulling the wings and legs off of flies--eh?

@Shimstu
There was a BIG@SS kerfuffle about SOLI just yesterday ( or maybe Friday).
@Mac Bro @Times Rita @Larg @Jennifer

@Helen Wright

Sal Mineo was a teen star in movies like Rebel Without A Cause.  He also played Dov Landau in Exodus.  (Oscar-nominated for Best Supporting actor for both.) He was tragically murdered at age 37.

Here's a clip of a famous scene that might jog your memory!  <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wBSEu6j27jo" target="_blank">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wBSEu6j27jo</a>

@Chungclan

I'm a thesis supervisor, among other things, so I can't help myself with this sort of stuff.

Then again... There was this one guy in Germany 10 years ago or so who was into cannibalism and he actually let another person kiII and eat him. Maybe that was a non-tragic kind of murder?

@Chungclan
Thanks for that. I’ve never actually watched the film. I know it’s an iconic US movie, but maybe because I was born some years after it was made, it was just another ‘old’ film to me.
Still baffled why I know his name.

@Jane Wheelaghan 

I figured you would have an edge today.

I saw early on that they weren't anagrams and happily threw that idea away. The thought of writing down the clues and crossing out letters as I used them didn't appeal to me.

@NYCC and @Aver

Apologies accepted. 

Honestly I'm not sure why I enjoy it but I do. How else would I ever have learned 43 different words for "stew"?

@Mean Old Lady
Nadiya was in season 3 of the British "Baking Show" when shown on PBS in 2015, which, confusingly, was the 6th season of the show in Great Britain. She's gone on to have quite a career over there. 
Also, I thought of you when ELAINE STRITCH came to light.

@Darren

4 out of 10 and you think it’s about “letter blanks”?  That aren’t used at all?

Make sure you correctly read the explanation and you understand the theme before you pronounce it a lousy puzzle.

@Andrzej
@Ιασων

TUDE and UIE are both dandy Murican speech.

Don't be jealous.

@Ιασων
SAMMIE is sillier.
I once walked out of a hipster lunch spot because the menu was all 'sammies'. Ugh.

@Joe
Thank you for the Latin. I didn't know it, but expect I'll have use for it.

Probably during my next family trip. (Two siblings will whip out their phones to search and the third will know what it is.)

@Rich in Atlanta
Pearls duly clutched.
Have added to my list of puzzles to try.

@Charles Peterson

Can we all just stop with the SOLI unless we're speaking Italian?

Basta!

@Steve L
Lord of the Rings is 25 years old. The olds were not that old then.
Surprised that no one has  complained about Greg Kinnear/REGINA KING. Are the crosses better?

@Billy

It is funny that two clues that upset people were really easy for me today.  I had problems with other things. We all have different strengths. 

I loved seeing LIAM NEESON and MORGAN FREEMAN as clues.

@HeathieJ
Nicely said! You express my own sentiments better than I did.

(A cruise!!! Enjoy.)

@Mike

Those letters but no other letters. If you read the column, you would see that it's called letter banking. 

Not that you have to like it, of course, but it is still a theme. They were actual anagrams nothing about the clue what it likely related to the answer either.

@Bill

Fortunately, because I took three years of Latin in high school, so I know exactly what this means.  But...

who said anything about a "turquoise pencil box"?

@Bill
I pasted your text into Google and instead of translating, Google AI responded:

"Visne ut hoc dictum in formam magis poeticam vel facetam redigam, an de grammatica Latina plura quaerere mavis?"

@Bill
When I gave it "de gustibus" earlier, it just translated it. 

Maybe it is guessing how advanced this Latin student is in order to figure out what kind of cheating to offer.
